Make file2alias's uuid_t survive a Darwin host.

file2alias defines its own uuid_t -- a struct with a b[16] member -- because
the kernel's is a struct and a Linux C library has no opinion about the name.
Darwin's does: <sys/types.h> defines uuid_t as unsigned char[16] and it arrives
through <stdio.h> before this file's own definition, so the typedef is a
redefinition with a different type and every access through the tee device id's
uuid field is rejected as a member reference into an array.

Both cannot exist in one translation unit. The SDK's is kept out through the
guard macro its own header honours, and the struct form is defined in its place
before any system header is read -- first rather than merely early, because
<unistd.h> reaches this file through modpost.h and declares functions taking a
uuid_t, which needs one in scope by then.

All of it is inside #ifdef __APPLE__; on any other host the file is unchanged.

--- a/scripts/mod/file2alias.c	2026-09-06 14:47:28
+++ b/scripts/mod/file2alias.c	2026-09-06 14:47:28
@@ -8,7 +8,19 @@
  *
  * This software may be used and distributed according to the terms
  * of the GNU General Public License, incorporated herein by reference.
+ */
+
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+/*
+ * Darwin defines uuid_t as unsigned char[16], and this file needs the kernel's
+ * struct form. _UUID_T is the guard the SDK's own header honours, so defining
+ * it here -- before <stdio.h> pulls <sys/types.h> in -- leaves the name to us,
+ * and defining the type immediately keeps the SDK declarations that take a
+ * uuid_t compiling.
  */
+#define _UUID_T
+typedef struct { unsigned char b[16]; } uuid_t;
+#endif
 
 #include <stdarg.h>
 #include <stdio.h>
@@ -107,9 +119,11 @@
 	__u8 b[16];
 } guid_t;
 
+#ifndef _UUID_T
 typedef struct {
 	__u8 b[16];
 } uuid_t;
+#endif
 
 #define	UUID_STRING_LEN		36
 
